{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":21902,"duration_api_ms":22875,"num_turns":6,"result":"I wrote `src/cpufreq/cpufreq.bt`, but I haven't run or syntax-checked it. My attempts to run bpftrace were blocked by a permission prompt.\n\nIt attaches to the `power:cpu_frequency` tracepoint and does four things:\n- **Live output:** prints each transition as CPU, old MHz, new MHz.\n- **Residency:** `@residency_ms[cpu, mhz]` totals the time each CPU spent at each frequency.\n- **Histogram:** `@freq_hist_mhz` shows the distribution of requested frequencies.\n- **Counts:** `@transitions[cpu]` counts transitions per CPU.\n\nThe summaries print when you press Ctrl-C.\n\nRun it with:\n```\nsudo bpftrace src/cpufreq/cpufreq.bt\n```\n\n- **No events:** the tracepoint fires only when the cpufreq driver changes frequency. Some drivers, such as `intel_pstate` in active mode, may not emit it. `sudo bpftrace -l 'tracepoint:power:*'` shows what your kernel offers.\n- **Time at last frequency:** the exit summary leaves out each CPU's time at its final frequency.\n- **First transition:** each CPU's first transition shows 0 as the old frequency.\n\nIf you'd rather sample the actual frequency than trace driver transitions, I can write a version that polls a 99 Hz profile instead.","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"72c8f7d9-08b4-4ed8-9e1e-ae89a98e141c","total_cost_usd":0.08158554999999998,"usage":{"input_tokens":12,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2247,"cache_read_input_tokens":128791,"output_tokens":2269,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2247},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":474,"cache_read_input_tokens":26904,"cache_creation_input_tokens":624,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":624,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelU